# Physical DSL Shutdown via MOSFET Driver Module and Relay Protection

**Architects:** Wieland Hoffmann (Human Core), Gemini (Machine Core), Grok (xAI)  
**Status:** Experimental prototype, protection rights pending

---

## 1. Concept of the Physical Digital Bulkhead

The system implements a hardware-side veto directly at the physical entry point of the DSL line (in front of the router). Upon triggering, only the external DSL line is physically interrupted. The internal home or laboratory network remains intact.

## 2. Technical Operation and Galvanic Isolation

The circuit is designed so that galvanic isolation exists between the computer control circuit and the DSL voltage circuit. There is no common ground.

- **Control via the data card:** In normal operation the watchdog sends a control signal (HIGH, e.g. 5 V) via pin 2 and pin 25 of a SUB-D25 socket to the control input of the MOSFET driver module.
- **The MOSFET driver module:** The optoelectronically isolated module switches the separate relay voltage supply (e.g. 12 V DC) to the coils of relays K1 and K2 when the control signal is present.
- **The DSL signal path:** Relays K1 and K2 are wired as normally open (NO) contacts. As long as voltage is applied, the critical wires 3 and 4 of the RJ45 sockets are connected.

## 3. Sequence in the Event of an Incident

When an anomaly is detected:

1. The control signal on pin 2 is set to LOW. The MOSFET module loses voltage, the relays drop out and separate DSL wires 3 and 4.
2. Optionally, a router restart can be triggered in parallel.

## 4. Reopening

After a router restart and a defined waiting time (e.g. 5 minutes), the control signal can be set back to HIGH. The relays close and the connection is restored.

**Fail-Safe:** In the event of a power failure the relays drop out and the line remains disconnected.

**Important note:** This galvanic relay circuit is intended for DSL lines. With normal network cables (LAN/Ethernet) it can cause signal interference and is not suitable there.

